<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/advanced/galaxies-and-the-expanding-universe">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A century ago, astronomers debated whether other galaxies existed. Today we know of
    <strong>trillions</strong>, arranged in filaments and voids — and their light tells us the
    universe is <strong>expanding</strong>, and was once hot, dense and small. The two decisive
    measurements — Hubble's recession law and the cosmic microwave background — turned the Big
    Bang from speculation into standard cosmology.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four discoveries that built modern cosmology" widget="equationStep" config={galaxiesCfg} />

  <h2>Reading the expansion</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Redshift is the measurement:</strong> expansion stretches light's wavelength; the farther the galaxy, the redder it arrives. Hubble's constant: ~70 km/s per megaparsec — meaning space roughly doubles every ~10 billion years.</li>
    <li><strong>Everything recedes from everything:</strong> the expansion has no centre — every observer sees the same Hubble flow, like raisins separating in rising dough.</li>
    <li><strong>Lookback time:</strong> seeing a galaxy 10 billion light-years away is seeing it 10 billion years ago. Telescopes are time machines; JWST's deep fields watch the first galaxies ignite a few hundred million years after the Big Bang.</li>
    <li><strong>Structure grew from ripples:</strong> tiny density ripples in the CMB (mapped by COBE, WMAP, Planck) became the seeds of galaxy clusters — the universe's largest structures were written in its smallest fluctuations.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hubble (1929); Penzias & Wilson (1965); Planck Collaboration (2018); OpenStax, Astronomy 2e (Galaxies).</p>
</TopicPage>
